.sh 2 "Protocols separate policy from transport"
.pp
Serializer and Deserializer adapt a user backend rather than prescribe a
wire format. A backend supplies serialize(value, output) and a typed
deserialize operation. The adapters borrow the backend and preserve its
return and error conventions. This lets a language tool use the same
interface for a binary cache, textual fixture, or application-owned transport
without pretending that every format shares one universal representation.
.pp
Abstract IO similarly borrows a backend with read and write operations on
byte buffers. Short transfers are valid. Read_exact and write_all loop until
their requested transfer completes, rejecting premature EOF, invalid counts,
or a write that makes no progress. Optional seek and tell operations exist
when the backend supports them. The caller must keep borrowed backends alive
and define how external resources are opened and closed.
.sh 2 "Binary streams make encoding choices explicit"
.pp
BinaryStream owns bytes and a cursor. Read consumes a requested interval;
write appends; integer operations take an explicit byte order. Load and save
connect the stream to abstract IO, with a size bound for loading. This is
the native substrate used by MetaML's Buffer module. Owning a byte buffer
does not automatically validate the protocol encoded in those bytes: widths,
magic values, checksums, and length relationships remain domain invariants.
.lua
book.algorithm("Decode a bounded binary record", {
    "Read and validate the fixed header before allocating payload storage.",
    "Decode lengths with explicit integer widths and byte order.",
    "Check arithmetic and the application size limit.",
    "Read exactly the declared payload length, handling short transfers.",
    "Validate payload structure and checksum if the format requires them.",
    "Publish a domain value only after every invariant holds."
})
.endlua
.pp
StaticRegex compiles a Thompson NFA at host compilation time and matches
bytes at runtime, including zero and high-bit bytes. Supported syntax includes
classes, alternation, grouping, repetition, anchors, and documented escapes.
There are no capture groups, backreferences, or arbitrary PCRE extensions.
Matches checks the whole input, prefix returns the longest match at a
position, and search chooses the earliest matching position with its longest
match. BinaryStream token consumption rejects empty matches.
.sh 2 "Preprocessing is source transformation"
.pp
Preprocessor supports object and function macros, balanced arguments,
recursive-expansion suppression, line continuation, includes, and conditional
blocks. It respects strings, character literals, and C-style comments.
Conditional expressions support a documented subset including defined,
comparisons, boolean operators, and integer constants. This is a DSL
preprocessor, not a complete implementation of the C or C++ preprocessor.
Token pasting, stringification, and variadic macros are not implied.
.pp
An include callback supplies source for a path, and a script callback
supplies generated source for a named language. Generated script output is
macro-expanded. Expansion and include depths are bounded. This callback
architecture keeps filesystem and scripting policy in the embedding host,
where resource ownership belongs. A source transformer should also preserve
or reconstruct provenance if diagnostics must refer to the original input.
.sh 2 "Optional Lua and the book's Lua are separate integrations"
.pp
The optional native Lua bridge uses DSLTK_ENABLE_LUA in C++ with appropriate
headers and linkage. D selects its corresponding DSLtkLua version flag.
The host owns the Lua state and assigns a script hook; each directive returns
a string, and the bridge restores the Lua stack after success or failure.
This facility is distinct from luaROFF, the separate document preprocessor
used to publish this book.
.pp
Both illustrate the same architectural idea: an embedded language generates
input for another stage through a well-defined boundary. Neither makes
generated text correct automatically. Validate generated grammar, source,
or markup at the next stage and retain errors from the generator. The
book build explicitly checks luaROFF diagnostics because its current
preprocessor can report some Lua failures without a failing exit status.
.sh 2 "Explicit CFGs support a different parsing model"
.pp
CFG has named nonterminals, token terminals, and productions. Its Earley
parser handles left recursion, epsilon productions, and unordered alternatives.
The user's lexer supplies tokens. A successful parse returns an AST for
the first discovered derivation of an ambiguous grammar; failure includes
a farthest token position and expected terminals. Returning one tree does
not prove the grammar has only one derivation.
.lua
book.code("text", [=[(grammar
  (start Expr)
  (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term))
  (rule Term ("number")))]=], "DSLtk's S-expression grammar notation")
.endlua
.pp
Quoted right-hand symbols denote token terminals and unquoted symbols denote
nonterminals. Each list is an alternative, with an empty list expressing
epsilon. Sexpr_grammar loads and validates that notation. Native CFGSymbol
and CFGRule values or template production helpers offer another construction
route. Production names become AST tags and terminals become token leaves,
so semantic visitors must account for that actual output vocabulary.
.sh 2 "Select interfaces by invariants"
.pp
These facilities fit together but should not be flattened into one giant
frontend. A lexer establishes token boundaries, a CFG establishes syntactic
structure, a visitor checks domain invariants, and a serializer defines an
external representation. Preprocessing may alter source before lexing but
must not conceal errors. Binary transport may deliver bytes but cannot
prove their grammar. Clear boundaries make each subsystem replaceable and
its failures understandable.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read the serialization, IO, StaticRegex, BinaryStream, Preprocessor, and CFG
sections of DSLtk.hpp and DSLtk.d. The toolkit manual chapters on codecs,
preprocessing, and context-free grammars provide paired native examples.
Design a small cache format for validated expression trees, then specify
its load limit, byte order, version marker, and decoding failures. Separately
design a left-recursive grammar and explain why its Earley parsing contract
cannot simply be assumed for an ordinary recursive-descent combinator.
